[?2004l[?2004h>         "qid": "b2531657ba5c7a78",
[?2004l[?2004h>         "status": "ANSWERED",
[?2004lo[?2004h>         "answer": "Paxlovid is associated with significant drug interactions because it includes ritonavir, a strong CYP3A inhibitor. Initiation of Paxlovid in patients receiving medications metabolized by CYP3A, or initiation of medications metabolized by CYP3A in patients already receiving Paxlovid, can increase the plasma concentrations of these concomitant medications, potentially resulting in severe, life-threatening, or fatal events. The most commonly reported concomitant medications resulting in serious adverse reactions were calcineurin inhibitors (e.g., tacrolimus, cyclosporine), followed by calcium channel blockers. Conversely, co-administration of Paxlovid with strong CYP3A inducers is contraindicated, as it may decrease plasma concentrations of nirmatrelvir and ritonavir, leading to a loss of therapeutic effect and the potential development of viral resistance. Additionally, Paxlovid is an inhibitor of CYP2D6, P-gp, and OATP1B1, meaning that co-administration with substrates of these enzymes or transporters may result in increased plasma concentrations and adverse events.",
[?2004l[?2004h>         "citations": [
[?2004l[?2004h>             {
[?2004lificantl[?2004h>                 "set_id": "8a99d6d6-fd9e-45bb-b1bf-48c7f761232a",
[?2004l[?2004h>                 "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0000",
[?2004lrol[?2004h>                 "section": "WARNING: SIGNIFICANT DRUG INTERACTIONS WITH PAXLOVID",
[?2004l[?2004h>                 "supporting_quote": "PAXLOVID includes ritonavir, a strong CYP3A inhibitor, which may lead to greater exposure of certain concomitant medications, resulting in potentially severe, life-threatening, or fatal events"
[?2004l[?2004h>             },
[?2004lith[?2004h>             {
[?2004l[?2004h>                 "set_id": "8a99d6d6-fd9e-45bb-b1bf-48c7f761232a",
[?2004lts[?2004h>                 "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0013",
[?2004l[?2004h>                 "section": "5.1 Risk of Serious Adverse Reactions Due to Drug Interactions",
[?2004l[?2004h>                 "supporting_quote": "Severe, life-threatening, and/or fatal adverse reactions due to drug interactions have been reported in patients treated with PAXLOVID. The most commonly reported concomitant medications resulting in serious adverse reactions were calcineurin inhibitors (e.g., tacrolimus, cyclosporine), followed by calcium channel blockers."
[?2004l[?2004h>             },
[?2004ln[?2004h>             {
[?2004l[?2004h>                 "set_id": "8a99d6d6-fd9e-45bb-b1bf-48c7f761232a",
[?2004lvir [?2004h>                 "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0019",
[?2004l[?2004h>                 "section": "7.1 Potential for PAXLOVID to Affect Other Drugs",
[?2004l[?2004h>                 "supporting_quote": "PAXLOVID (nirmatrelvir co-packaged with ritonavir) is a strong inhibitor of CYP3A, and an inhibitor of CYP2D6, P-gp and OATP1B1."
[?2004lote[?2004h>             },
[?2004l[?2004h>             {
[?2004l[?2004h>                 "set_id": "8a99d6d6-fd9e-45bb-b1bf-48c7f761232a",
[?2004l[?2004h>                 "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0020",
[?2004l[?2004h>                 "section": "7.2 Potential for Other Drugs to Affect PAXLOVID",
[?2004l[?2004h>                 "supporting_quote": "Nirmatrelvir and ritonavir are CYP3A substrates; therefore, drugs that induce CYP3A may decrease nirmatrelvir and ritonavir plasma concentrations and reduce PAXLOVID therapeutic effect ."
[?2004l[?2004h>             }
[?2004l[?2004h>         ],
[?2004l[?2004h>         "structured_fields": {}
[?2004l[?2004h>     },
[?2004l[?2004h>     {
[?2004l[?2004h>         "qid": "6f12607a1ba14dd5",
[?2004l[?2004h>         "status": "ANSWERED",
[?2004l[?2004h>         "answer": "Medications that should be avoided (contraindicated) with Paxlovid include: 1) Drugs primarily metabolized by CYP3A for which elevated concentrations are associated with serious and/or life-threatening reactions: alfuzosin (alpha 1-adrenoreceptor antagonist); ranolazine (antianginal); amiodarone, dronedarone, flecainide, propafenone, quinidine (antiarrhythmics); colchicine in patients with renal and/or hepatic impairment (anti-gout); lurasidone, pimozide (antipsychotics); silodosin (benign prostatic hyperplasia agent); eplerenone, ivabradine (cardiovascular agents); dihydroergotamine, ergotamine, methylergonovine (ergot derivatives); lovastatin, simvastatin (HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors); voclosporin (immunosuppressant); lomitapide (microsomal triglyceride transfer protein inhibitor); eletriptan, ubrogepant (migraine medications); finerenone (mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist); naloxegol (opioid antagonist); sildenafil [Revatio] when used for pulmonary arterial hypertension (PDE5 inhibitor); triazolam, oral midazolam (sedative/hypnotics); flibanserin (serotonin receptor 1A agonist/2A antagonist); and tolvaptan (vasopressin receptor antagonist). 2) Strong CYP3A inducers where significantly reduced nirmatrelvir or ritonavir plasma concentrations may lead to loss of virologic response and resistance: apalutamide, enzalutamide (anticancer drugs); carbamazepine, phenobarbital, primidone, phenytoin (anticonvulsants); rifampin, rifapentine (antimycobacterials); lumacaftor/ivacaftor (cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator potentiator); and St. John's Wort (herbal product).",
[?2004l[?2004h>         "citations": [
[?2004l[?2004h>             {
[?2004l[?2004h>                 "set_id": "8a99d6d6-fd9e-45bb-b1bf-48c7f761232a",
[?2004l[?2004h>                 "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0012",
[?2004l[?2004h>                 "section": "4 CONTRAINDICATIONS",
[?2004l[?2004h>                 "supporting_quote": "PAXLOVID is contraindicated with drugs that are primarily metabolized by CYP3A and for which elevated concentrations are associated with serious and/or life-threatening reactions and drugs that are strong CYP3A inducers where significantly reduced nirmatrelvir or ritonavir plasma concentrations may be associated with the potential for loss of virologic response and possible resistance."
[?2004l[?2004h>             }
[?2004lGE[?2004h>         ],
[?2004l[?2004h>         "structured_fields": {}
[?2004l[?2004h>     },
[?2004l Do[?2004h>     {
[?2004l[?2004h>         "qid": "daef650b92e56473",
[?2004l[?2004h>         "status": "ANSWERED",
[?2004ler mL Oral Sol[?2004h>         "answer": "Lopinavir and ritonavir oral solution must be taken with food. When administered via a feeding tube, it is not recommended for use with polyurethane feeding tubes due to potential incompatibility with the excipients ethanol and propylene glycol. Feeding tubes made of materials compatible with these excipients, such as silicone and polyvinyl chloride (PVC), can be used for administration. In pediatric patients, the dose of the oral solution should be administered using the supplied calibrated cup or an oral dosing syringe.",
[?2004l[?2004h>         "citations": [
[?2004l[?2004h>             {
[?2004lu[?2004h>                 "set_id": "4b1caefa-e6a2-42f3-9194-6dc1dd9e6d85",
[?2004l[?2004h>                 "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0001",
[?2004l[?2004h>                 "section": "2.1 General Administration Recommendations",
[?2004l[?2004h>                 "supporting_quote": "Lopinavir and ritonavir oral solution must be taken with food."
[?2004l    [?2004h>             },
[?2004lct[?2004h>             {
[?2004lion": "2.5 Dos[?2004h>                 "set_id": "4b1caefa-e6a2-42f3-9194-6dc1dd9e6d85",
[?2004lpporting_quo[?2004h>                 "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0002",
[?2004l[?2004h>                 "section": "2.2 Administering Oral Solution by Feeding Tube",
[?2004lassocia[?2004h>                 "supporting_quote": "Because lopinavir and ritonavir oral solution contains ethanol and propylene glycol, it is not recommended for use with polyurethane feeding tubes due to potential incompatibility. Feeding tubes that are compatible with ethanol and propylene glycol, such as silicone and polyvinyl chloride (PVC) feeding tubes, can be used for administration of lopinavir and ritonavir oral solution."
[?2004l[?2004h>             },
[?2004l[?2004h>             {
[?2004l[?2004h>                 "set_id": "4b1caefa-e6a2-42f3-9194-6dc1dd9e6d85",
[?2004l[?2004h>                 "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0007",
[?2004l[?2004h>                 "section": "2.4 Dosage Recommendations in Pediatric Patients",
[?2004l[?2004h>                 "supporting_quote": "The dose of the oral solution should be administered using the calibrated cup (supplied) or oral dosing syringe."
[?2004l[?2004h>             }
[?2004l[?2004h>         ],
[?2004led tran[?2004h>         "structured_fields": {}
[?2004lsaminases, hepatic decompensatio[?2004h>     },
[?2004l and [?2004h>     {
[?2004l[?2004h>         "qid": "a0576a26f2a2eeac",
[?2004l[?2004h>         "status": "ANSWERED",
[?2004l[?2004h>         "answer": "Lopinavir and ritonavir can be administered as a once-daily or twice-daily dosing regimen in adults. 1) Once-daily dosing: The recommended dosage is 800 mg/200 mg (10 mL) once daily. Once-daily dosing is NOT recommended in pediatric patients younger than 18 years, pregnant women, in combination with carbamazepine, phenobarbital, phenytoin, efavirenz, nevirapine, or nelfinavir, or in adult patients with three or more lopinavir resistance-associated substitutions. 2) Twice-daily dosing: The recommended dosage is 400 mg/100 mg (5 mL) twice daily. The dose must be increased to 520 mg/130 mg (6.5 mL) twice daily when administered in combination with efavirenz, nevirapine, or nelfinavir. 3) Pregnancy: Administer 400 mg/100 mg twice daily in pregnant patients with no documented lopinavir-associated resistance substitutions. Once-daily dosing is not recommended in pregnancy, and use of the oral solution should be avoided. 4) Pediatric patients: Once-daily dosing is not recommended. The oral solution is not recommended in neonates before a postmenstrual age of 42 weeks and a postnatal age of at least 14 days has been attained.",
[?2004l[?2004h>         "citations": [
[?2004l[?2004h>             {
[?2004le":[?2004h>                 "set_id": "4b1caefa-e6a2-42f3-9194-6dc1dd9e6d85",
[?2004lud[?2004h>                 "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0003",
[?2004l[?2004h>                 "section": "2.3 Dosage Recommendations in Adults",
[?2004l[?2004h>                 "supporting_quote": "Lopinavir and ritonavir can be given in once daily or twice daily dosing regimen at dosages noted in Tables 1 and 2."
[?2004l[?2004h>             },
[?2004l[?2004h>             {
